# One worktree per agent

Concurrent agents work in this repo. Do not edit, commit, or run `git checkout`
in the checkout you were opened in — another agent may be mid-turn there. Before
the first edit, add your own worktree of **this** repo and work from it.

```bash
git fetch origin
git worktree add -b <topic> ../orbweaver-worktrees/<slug> origin/main
cd ../orbweaver-worktrees/<slug>
```

The path is a sibling of the repo root (`/home/spark/orbweaver-worktrees/<slug>`),
so no agent's `Grep`/`Glob` walks another agent's tree. Branch off `origin/main`,
per the feature-branch flow in `ORBWEAVER.md`; that flow — version bump, tests,
draft PR, squash auto-merge — then runs from inside the worktree.

Pass the worktree as `working_directory` (or `git -C`) on every command, because
the shell's own cwd still starts at the original checkout.

Read-only work — answering questions, reading code, inspecting logs — needs no
worktree.

## Do not

- Clone the repo, or switch the agent's workspace root, to get isolation.
  `git worktree add` in the current repo is the whole mechanism.
- Touch, reset, or `git worktree remove` a worktree you did not create.
- Reuse someone else's topic branch. One worktree, one branch, one task.
